is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the three core routers’ OSPF adjacencies and underlying links. All core-facing interfaces are physically up (ethtool shows link detected: yes on router_core_1 eth0/eth1, router_core_2 eth0/eth1, router_core_3 eth0/eth1) with no drops/overlimits (tc fq_codel stats all zero drops). End-to-end reachability from campus hosts to the server farm shows 0% packet loss and low RTTs.

OSPF is converged and stable: all core neighbors are Full, with consistent timers (Hello 10s/Dead 40s) and no outstanding LSDB queues. Current OSPF neighbor “Up Time” on the cores is about a minute, and FRR logs show the last adjacency formations around 12:20:31 (e.g., router_core_2: “Packet[DD]: Neighbor 172.16.0.1 Negotiation done (Master)”). Each neighbor reports about six state changes, which matches the normal sequence of states during initial adjacency establishment. There is no evidence of ongoing flaps: dead timers are counting down normally, and no recent interface carrier changes appear in dmesg.

Localization: OSPF on router_core_1 eth0/eth1, router_core_2 eth0/eth1, and router_core_3 eth0/eth1. Mechanism: a one-time FRR/OSPF restart or initial lab bring-up caused brief adjacency drops and re-formation, which monitoring likely interpreted as “core links bouncing.”

Root cause: transient OSPF adjacency re-establishment during recent FRR startup, not a persistent fault. Corrective fix: none required. Optionally, set OSPF network type to point-to-point on /31 core links to eliminate DR/BDR chatter and tune monitoring thresholds to ignore brief convergence at startup. The core is steady now. 
=== END ===